<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The gateway runs on a laptop reached over the phone's own hotspot (or, for USB-tethered
  bench testing, over `adb reverse` via 127.0.0.1), on plain HTTP and ws://. Android
  blocks cleartext by default, so it is opted in explicitly here rather than left off.

  NOT scoped to RFC1918 domain entries; an earlier version of this file tried that
  (a literal "domain" entry per octet, e.g. 192.168.0.0) on the mistaken assumption
  that Android's network-security-config domain matching supports CIDR ranges. It does
  not: a domain entry is an exact hostname/IP-literal match (or exact-suffix with
  includeSubdomains), so 192.168.0.0 only ever matched that one literal address, never
  the laptop's actual DHCP-leased IP on the hotspot subnet. That silently blocked
  cleartext on every real network, not just this bench test; there is no evidence it
  was ever exercised end to end before now.

  The actual trust boundary is that the server URL is never hardcoded or auto-discovered;
  it is whatever the operator types into the app. Cleartext is permitted globally so
  that boundary is the one actually enforced, rather than a private-range filter that
  cannot be expressed in this format and was silently doing nothing.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />
</network-security-config>
